course: dictionary
lesson: 4478
title: Harsh and its many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: One word can have several meanings, and context tells you which one the writer means. Harsh can mean cruel or too strict, very hard to live in, rough and unpleasant to the senses, or, in slang, unfair. «The winter was harsh, and the village had little food.» Winter is not a person, so harsh must mean very hard to live in.
explain: Look at the people and things around the word. «Tom went to the river, and now Mum wants a text every time he arrives anywhere; it feels harsh.» Mum is being very strict, so harsh means too strict here. Ask yourself: what word would fit? Then try your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Harsh can also describe how something feels or sounds. «The harsh light hurt my eyes» and «a harsh voice» are about things that are too bright, loud or rough. Slang is different again: when a friend says «That's harsh, mate!», they mean that is unfair. The clues around the word show you which meaning to use.
fact: Harsh means cruel, unkind or too strict.
fact: «Harsh» can also mean very hard to live in, like «a harsh winter» or «a harsh desert».
fact: «Harsh» can describe light, sound or rough things that are unpleasant to the senses.
fact: In slang, «That's harsh!» means that is unfair.
fact: Harsh is an adjective, and it has an adverb: harshly.
exercise: «The winter was harsh, and the village had little food.» What does harsh mean here? | *Very hard to live in | Quiet and peaceful | Bright and colourful | Warm and sunny
exercise: Sam says «That's harsh, mate!» when his teacher gives extra homework for being one minute late. What does he mean? | That's a long way to walk | That's very loud | *That's unfair | That's a great idea
exercise: Which phrase is about unkind words? | warm sun | *harsh words | green hills | soft rain
test: «The harsh desert has very little water.» What does harsh mean? | Soft and gentle | Cruel and unkind | *Very hard to live in | Too bright to look at
test: Why does Tom feel that Mum's texting rule is harsh? | *It is very strict, and he must text every time he arrives | It is a big prize he has won | It is a surprise party | It is a quiet and soft song
exam: The TV judges were harsh about the singer's song, and the comments made her cry; what does harsh most likely mean? | *Cruel and unkind | Very quiet and soft | Hot and sunny | Cheerful and kind
